    overview:
      "Welcome to property law. This field governs the rules for who owns what, who may possess it, who may use someone else's land, and what happens when those rights collide or when money is borrowed against them. Over the next forty minutes you will work through ten ideas in roughly the order a property dispute actually surfaces them. We start with the basic vocabulary of ownership itself: what a person actually holds when they 'own' land, how that holding can be divided in time and shared among several owners at once, and how a transfer of that holding is actually made and protected against competing claims. We then turn to the two doctrines that let a stranger's use of land ripen into a right of its own, adverse possession and the law of easements and covenants, before moving into the two relationships that generate the largest volume of real-world property disputes, the landlord-tenant relationship and the mortgage. We close with two practical classification questions that recur constantly in practice, whether an item is part of the land or still personal property, and what a landowner can do when a neighbor's own use of their own land interferes with yours. Keep one distinction running underneath all of it: property law protects both possession and ownership, and they are not the same thing, so the first question in almost any dispute is which of the two is actually being protected.",
