        explanation:
          "American property law starts from a deceptively simple idea: 'owning' land is really owning a bundle of rights that can be sliced along a timeline, with different people holding different slices of the same physical parcel at the same time. The fee simple absolute is the largest estate the law recognizes, potentially infinite in duration with no strings attached. Below it sit defeasible fees, a fee simple determinable, which ends automatically the instant a stated condition occurs, and a fee simple subject to a condition subsequent, which the grantor must affirmatively act to cut short once the condition occurs, a distinction that turns entirely on the deed's own wording ('so long as' versus 'but if'). A life estate lasts only for a specified life, often the grantee's own, and whoever eventually takes the property afterward holds a future interest: a reversion if the original grantor keeps that future right, or a remainder if a third party was given it instead, itself further divided into vested remainders, certain to take effect, and contingent remainders, dependent on an event or an unascertained person. A fee simple that cuts short a prior estate held by someone other than the grantor is an executory interest rather than a remainder. Crucially, American law operates on a numerus clausus: courts will not enforce a brand-new, custom-invented form of estate that does not fit one of this fixed, limited menu of recognized categories, however clearly the parties intended it. The Rule Against Perpetuities further polices contingent future interests, voiding any interest that might not vest within lives in being plus twenty-one years, a rule many states have since relaxed or abolished by statute but that still shapes how careful drafters structure a trust or a conditional gift of land today.",
