    connections:
      "Ten ideas, one through-line: property law is the law of who holds which slice of a parcel, for how long, and against whom. Estates and concurrent ownership answer the baseline question of what is actually held and by how many people at once, and deeds and recording acts govern how that holding changes hands and how a new owner's claim is protected against a rival one. Adverse possession and easements show how a stranger's use, not a formal grant at all, can ripen into an ownership right or a lesser right to use land anyway, while covenants and servitudes show the opposite movement, an owner voluntarily giving up part of their own freedom of use to bind every future owner who comes after them. Landlord-tenant law and mortgages are where estates, recording, and remedies all meet the two relationships that generate the largest share of real-world property litigation, a possessory leasehold carved out of a larger estate, and a security interest layered on top of one. Fixtures close the loop by asking which objects count as part of the land those first nine concepts have all been analyzing, and nuisance shows that even an owner's fullest, most unencumbered estate has never been a license to do anything at all with it, regardless of what zoning happens to separately permit. Notice, across all ten, the same recurring tension: a strong default rule protecting settled expectations, record title, the four unities, habitability, against a narrower doctrine that intervenes when settled expectations clash with actual facts on the ground, possession, use, necessity, harm. Property disputes are almost always a fight over which of those two forces should win on a particular set of facts.",
