        explanation:
          "Transferring ownership of land in Germany requires two further, cumulative steps beyond the underlying sale contract: Auflassung under §925 BGB, a specific, formal real-property conveyance agreement that, unlike an ordinary contract, must be declared by both parties in each other's simultaneous presence, almost always before a Notar, and Eintragung, entry in the Grundbuch, the land register, under §873 BGB, without which ownership simply does not pass regardless of what the parties otherwise agreed. This dual requirement reflects the Publizitätsprinzip, the principle of publicity, the idea that real rights in land should be visible and verifiable in a public register rather than resting on private, unrecorded agreements. §892 BGB then layers on öffentlicher Glaube des Grundbuchs, the register's public reliability: a buyer who acquires in good faith, relying on what the Grundbuch actually shows, generally takes free of an unregistered defect in the seller's title, even if the register turns out to have been objectively wrong, unless the buyer actually knew of the discrepancy or a formal objection, Widerspruch, was already noted against the entry.",
