    connections:
      "Ten ideas, one underlying structure. The Trennungs- and Abstraktionsprinzip set the analytical frame for everything that follows: a property question in German law is never just 'what did the parties agree,' it is always, separately, 'did the real right itself actually and validly transfer,' and Eigentum and Besitz show why that second question matters even independent of ownership, since possession carries its own, separately enforceable protection. The Grundbuch and Auflassung are where that real transfer of land actually happens and is made publicly reliable, and Buchersitzung shows just how narrow the exception to that registry-centered system actually is, confirming that German law channels almost every title question back through the register rather than through decades of unregistered physical use. Dienstbarkeiten and the mortgage instruments show the numerus clausus at work on both sides of a property relationship, the fixed menu of lesser rights that can burden land and the two different ways a debt can be secured against it, while Mietrecht shows the same codified, specific-provision approach applied to one of the most common and heavily regulated property relationships of all. Bestandteile and Zubehör close the loop on what actually counts as 'the land' in the first place, and Nachbarrecht shows that even full, unencumbered Eigentum under §903 has never meant a landowner can ignore its neighbors, only that German law resolves that tension through specific, codified duties rather than one open-ended balancing test.",
