          "Residential tenancy in Sweden is governed by Jordabalken Chapter 12, universally called Hyreslagen, the Rent Act, even though it is formally part of the Land Code itself, and two features dominate Swedish tenancy practice specifically. The bruksvärdessystemet, utility-value system, caps what rent a landlord may actually charge for a given apartment by reference to comparable apartments, in practice overwhelmingly apartments owned by municipal, not-for-profit housing companies, whose negotiated rents effectively set the benchmark the whole private rental market is measured against, rather than letting rent float freely with whatever the market alone would bear. Besittningsskydd, security of tenure, gives a tenant a strong, statutorily protected right to remain and have the tenancy renewed indefinitely, and a landlord seeking to end a tenancy against the tenant's wishes must establish one of a specific, closed list of statutory grounds before the Hyresnämnden, the specialized rent tribunal, rather than simply declining to renew at the end of a fixed term the way a more lightly regulated system might permit.",
